  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">US&#x2013;OCHA Funding Allocation for Uganda</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The project employs a dual-sector approach, with 50% of resources dedicated to Child Protection and 50% to VAWG. The strategic focus is on providing survivor-centered case management, establishing and maintaining safe spaces for women and children, strengthening community-based protection mechanisms (CBPMs), and providing targeted Cash and Voucher Assistance (CVA) for protection outcomes. This integrated model is necessitated by the 2025 surge in protection incidents, where Child Protection cases increased by 37% and Gender-Based Violence (GBV) cases rose by 32% amid a general decline in service capacity. By focusing on transit centers and settlements with Severity 4 and 5 needs, the project provides a vital safety net for new arrivals and those in protracted displacement who are currently facing the risk of system collapse._x000d_
_x000d_
Project Objective_x000d_
To deliver urgent, life-saving protection assistance and comprehensive response services to 17,600 vulnerable children and survivors of violence against women and girls (8,544 girls, 3,932 boys, 5,040 women, and 84 men) in the refugee settlements, reception centers, and transit centers of Southwest Uganda, ensuring the preservation of safety, dignity, and access to essential rights through localized and integrated protection frameworks within a six-month emergency window. _x000d_
_x000d_
Locations: Kyangwali Settlement, Sebagoro Settlement, Kyaka II Settlement, Rwanwanja Settlement, Nakivale Settlement, Oruchinga Settlement, Bubukwanga Settlement, _x000d_
Matanda Settlement, Nyakabande Settlement.</narrative>
  </description>
